Opt-in analyses (off by default, so default output is unchanged): \code{ploidy_prior} (\code{FALSE}; G-14a)
replaces the rule that the lowest ladder level holding \code{comb_tooth_min_frac} of the genome is one copy
by a soft prior. Each admissible base copy number, from that rule's choice up to the largest purity <= 1
allows, gives a mean ploidy; the one with the highest density under a mixture of two normal
distributions is taken. The mixture has weight \code{ploidy_prior_wgd} (0.3; G-14a; about 30 \% of advanced
cancers are genome-doubled, Bielski et al. 2018 Nat Genet 50:1189) on a doubled-genome component with mean
\code{ploidy_prior_mu2} (3.31; G-14a), the rest on a diploid-like component with mean \code{ploidy_prior_mu1}
(1.99; G-14a; the two means are the mean ploidy of non-doubled tumours and of tumours after one doubling in
Zack et al. 2013 Nat Genet 45:1134), both with standard deviation \code{ploidy_prior_sd} (0.4; G-14a; the
developer's choice, not sourced). \code{ploidy_prior_mu1} and \code{ploidy_prior_mu2} must be finite, \code{ploidy_prior_sd} > 0,
\code{ploidy_prior_wgd} between 0 and 1 and \code{ploidy_prior_ambig} >= 1, or the run stops. \code{ploidy_prior_ratio} is \code{NA} where it would not be finite (the
runner-up's density underflows). If even the best candidate's density underflows to 0 the prior gave no
information: the hard choice is kept, the ratio is \code{NA} and \code{ploidy_ambiguous} is set. It can only
raise the base copy number, and applies only when the ladder is estimated (not with supplied purity and
ploidy). \code{ploidy_prior_ambig} (3; G-14b) sets the flag \code{ploidy_ambiguous}, raised when the best candidate
is less than this many times as probable as the runner-up. With the prior on, \code{samples} gains the columns
\code{ploidy_hard}, \code{ploidy_alt} and \code{ploidy_prior_ratio}; these are \code{NA} for tumours where the prior did not
run (a single admissible base copy number, supplied purity and ploidy, or a fallback ladder). On BriTROC-1 the
pre-registered benchmark found no improvement from the prior (\verb{docs/spec/08_britroc.md}, section 8).

Arm baselines (G-14c; the shared base of later opt-in analyses, so they change no default output):
\code{arm_baseline} (\code{"median"} or \code{"twolevel"}) says how the level an arm sits at is estimated. \code{"median"} (the
default, chosen by a pre-registered comparison on simulated data and BriTROC-1) is the marker-weighted median of the arm's segments (the lower one: the smallest value at which the cumulative
weight reaches half). \code{"twolevel"} fits each chromosome one level or two, split at a single breakpoint, by
least absolute deviations and the Bayesian information criterion with penalty (2k - 1) ln n for k levels (the
broad-level fit described in the Supplementary Methods of Mermel et al. 2011 Genome Biol 12:R41), and each arm takes the level
that covers most of its markers. \code{arm_min_markers} (50) is the number of markers an arm needs to be eligible.

Focal-only analysis (G-14d): \code{focal_only} (\code{FALSE}) scores each segment on its deviation from its arm's baseline,
so arm-level change leaves the scores and the significance test, and the null model is rebuilt from the same
focal scores. Segments crossing the centromere are first cut there (a segment with fewer than 2 markers is left
whole and judged on the arm of its start marker). The gain, amplification and loss detectors are evaluated at the residual: in copy units
kmode + (c - b) copies, kmode being the sample's rounded ploidy (at least 1), c the segment's and b the arm's copy
number; on the log2 scale (arrays, and samples whose copy step fell back) the segment mean minus b. The
homozygous-deletion detector stays on the absolute value, so a homozygous deletion on a lost arm keeps it. A run of
contiguous segments deviating in one direction over more than \code{focal_max_bp} (2e7, 20 Mb, the conventional upper
bound of a focal event) is scored 0 and its share of the sample's segment-covered span is written as
\code{focal_excluded_frac} in \code{samples}; such a run continues across the centromere, so a deviation spanning both arms
is one event. Focal (split segments, focal scores): the breakpoint profile, the null model, \code{regions}, \code{roi},
\code{calls}, \code{segments} and \code{n_segments}. Absolute, and equal to a run without \code{focal_only}: \code{rae_fga}, \code{fga} and \code{n_altered} (the cut keeps the
segment's bp length, and a cut pair is counted once). \code{focal_max_bp} must be > 0. With \code{build = "custom"} (no centromeres) each chromosome is one arm, and the run records a warning.

Arm-level cohort test (G-14e): \code{arm_test} (\code{FALSE}) tests whether each chromosome arm is altered in more
tumours than a random arm, given each tumour's own number of altered arms. Each tumour's four detectors are
evaluated at its arm baselines (in copy units through the ladder, otherwise on the log2 scale), the scores are
averaged over the tumours with data on the arm and combined as for regions (gain sqrt(A0^2 + A1^2), loss
sqrt(D0^2 + D1^2)). The null permutes each tumour's arm scores across the eligible arms (at least
\code{arm_min_markers} markers) \code{arm_n_perm} (10000) times, on its own random stream. The table, written as a ninth
file \verb{<prefix>.arms.tsv} and returned as \code{arms}, has pooled permutation p-values, Benjamini-Hochberg q and
Westfall-Young max-T adjusted p-values (\verb{fwer_*}) for gain and loss separately. In highly altered cohorts
(high-grade serous ovarian cancer, for example) the null mean is high, so the test finds the arms altered
more than the tumours' average arm. It changes no other output. \code{arm_n_perm} and \code{arm_min_markers} must be
at least 1. With \code{build = "custom"} each chromosome is one arm, and the run records a warning.
On the command line \code{--focal-max-mb} is in Mb while \code{focal_max_bp} is in bp, and \code{--arm-perm} sets \code{arm_n_perm}.

Independence of a secondary peak (G-14f): \code{peak_independence} (\code{FALSE}) asks, for an event with more than one
stage-2 peak, whether each later peak is more than the shoulder of a higher one. The peaks are taken in order of
score; the highest is the primary. For each later peak, every tumour's runs (maximal stretches of consecutive
segments on one chromosome whose detector score is at least \code{peak_run_min}, 0.5; for the linear A1 of the array
presets and of samples flagged \code{step_fallback}, \code{amp_threshold}) that reach the apex region of the primary or of a peak already found independent are set
to 0, component by component (A0 and A1 for gain, D0 and D1 for loss). The tumours' region means over the peak's
span are recomputed with the same weighting, and the residual summary score is their cohort mean combined as for
regions; \code{resid_score} is its maximum over the span and \code{resid_p} its p-value against the same null as the
observed scores. The peak is \code{independent} (1) when \code{resid_score} is at least the lowest observed score among
regions with q <= \code{peak_q}, and then joins the accepted set; otherwise 0. The primary peak, a peak alone in its
event and the event rows are \code{NA}. Under \code{focal_only} the runs use the focal scores, so they describe focal
events only, and they continue across the centromere. The idea is GISTIC2's peel-off (Mermel et al. 2011 Genome
Biol 12:R41), reimplemented from its published description; no GISTIC2 code is used. With the switch on, \code{roi}
gains the columns \code{resid_score}, \code{resid_p} and \code{independent}; nothing else changes. \code{peak_run_min} must be in
(0, 1]. Sub-threshold stretches (score below \code{peak_run_min}) are never removed, so a shoulder carried by weak
runs can still be called independent; raising \code{peak_run_min} is the control.
